The First Fifty Discourses
## 1. Chapter on Punishments (Kammakaraṇavagga)
## 1. Discourse on Faults (Vajjasutta)
1. Thus have I heard. On one occasion the Blessed One was dwelling at Sāvatthī in Jeta's Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika's Park. There the Blessed One addressed the bhikkhus: "Bhikkhus." "Venerable Sir," those bhikkhus replied to the Blessed One. The Blessed One said this:

"Bhikkhus, there are these two kinds of faults. What two? The fault in this life and the fault in the life to come. And what, bhikkhus, is the fault in this life? Here, bhikkhus, some person sees kings having caught a thief, a wrongdoer, and inflicting various punishments on him: having him flogged with whips, having him flogged with canes, having him flogged with rods, having his hands cut off, having his feet cut off, having his hands and feet cut off, having his ears cut off, having his nose cut off, having his ears and nose cut off;

having the 'gruel-pot' punishment (bilangathālika) inflicted, where the skull is broken open, a red-hot iron ball is taken with tongs and dropped into the head so that the brain boils over like rice gruel;

having the 'shell-shave' punishment (saṅkhamuṇḍika) inflicted, where the scalp is sliced from the upper lip on both sides of the temples down to the neck, the hair is twisted into a bundle with a stick and the scalp is pulled off, then it is scrubbed with gravel until the bare head is white like the color of a conch shell;

having the 'Rāhu's mouth' punishment (rāhumukha) inflicted, where the mouth is pried open with a spike and a lamp is lit inside so it looks like the mouth of Rāhu swallowing the moon;

having the 'fire-garland' punishment (jotimālika) inflicted, where the whole body is wrapped in cloth, doused with oil, and set on fire like a garland of flames;

having the 'hand-torch' punishment (hatthapajjotika) inflicted, where the hands are wrapped in cloth, doused with oil, and set on fire like hand-torches;

having the 'grass-wrap' punishment (erakavattika) inflicted, where the skin is flayed from the neck downwards and piled up at the ankles, and the person is pulled with a rope so that they trip and fall over their own skin again and again;

having the 'bark-dress' punishment (cīrakavāsika) inflicted, where the skin is flayed from the neck and placed at the waist, and from the waist and placed at the ankles, so that the upper skin covers the lower body like a garment of bark."

